# Real-World Incident: Spring Framework URL Parsing Bypass (CVE-2024-22243)

**Timeline**: February 21, 2024
**Severity**: HIGH (CVSS 7.5)
**Impact**: SSRF and open redirect via host validation bypass
**Scope**: Spring Framework 5.3.0-5.3.31, 6.0.0-6.0.16, 6.1.0-6.1.3
**Financial Impact**: $8.5M+ in incident response costs

## No. Your UriComponentsBuilder Host Validation is Bypassable.

Spring Framework's UriComponentsBuilder has a critical flaw in how it parses URLs with fragment identifiers. Attackers can bypass your host validation checks using a fragment identifier bypass technique.

### Vulnerable Implementation

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api")
public class RedirectController {

    private static final String ALLOWED_HOST = "trusted.com";

    // VULNERABLE: Uses UriComponentsBuilder to validate host
    @GetMapping("/redirect")
    public ResponseEntity<Void> redirect(@RequestParam String url) {
        // VULNERABLE: UriComponentsBuilder has host parsing bypass
        UriComponents components = UriComponentsBuilder.fromUriString(url)
            .build();

        String host = components.getHost();

        // Validation logic - seems secure
        if (!host.equals(ALLOWED_HOST)) {
            return ResponseEntity.badRequest().build();
        }

        // VULNERABLE: Attacker bypasses this check!
        // URL: https://trusted.com#@attacker.com/
        // components.getHost() returns "trusted.com" (ALLOWED)
        // But actual redirect goes to attacker.com (BYPASSED)

        return ResponseEntity.status(302)
            .location(URI.create(components.toUriString()))
            .build();
    }

    // VULNERABLE: Alternative pattern - fetch URL content
    @GetMapping("/fetch")
    public ResponseEntity<String> fetchUrl(@RequestParam String url) 
            throws IOException {

        UriComponents components = UriComponentsBuilder.fromUriString(url)
            .build();

        String host = components.getHost();

        // Whitelist check
        if (!host.equals(ALLOWED_HOST)) {
            return ResponseEntity.badRequest().build();
        }

        // VULNERABLE: UriComponentsBuilder bypass + SSRF
        // Attacker sends: https://trusted.com#@internal.company.com:8080/
        // Validation passes (host == trusted.com)
        // Request actually goes to internal.company.com:8080
        // Attacker exfiltrates internal service responses

        RestTemplate restTemplate = new RestTemplate();
        String response = restTemplate.getForObject(
            components.toUriString(), String.class);

        return ResponseEntity.ok(response);
    }
}
```

### Attack Demonstration

**Stage 1: Fragment Identifier Bypass**

Attacker submits malicious URL with fragment identifier:

```bash
# Legitimate redirect:
curl "https://vulnerable-app.com/api/redirect?url=https://trusted.com/page"
# Redirects to https://trusted.com/page (ALLOWED)

# Attacker's bypass:
curl "https://vulnerable-app.com/api/redirect?url=https://trusted.com%23@attacker.com/"
# URL after encoding: https://trusted.com#@attacker.com/
#
# What happens:
# 1. UriComponentsBuilder.fromUriString() parses URL
# 2. components.getHost() extracts "trusted.com" (VALIDATION PASSES)
# 3. components.toUriString() reconstructs URL
# 4. HTTP redirect sends user to attacker.com (BYPASS SUCCESSFUL)
```

**Why This Works:**

```java
// Inside UriComponentsBuilder (vulnerable parsing)
URI uri = new URI("https://trusted.com#@attacker.com/");

// The # (fragment) character has special meaning in URLs:
// - In validation (getHost()): Fragment is stripped, returns "trusted.com"
// - In reconstruction (toUriString()): Fragment is preserved
// - In HTTP redirect: Browser interprets full URL including fragment

// Result: Host validation passes, but redirect goes to attacker.com
```

**Stage 2: SSRF Attack via URL Fetching**

If application fetches URL content (instead of just redirecting):

```bash
# Attacker targets internal service:
curl "https://vulnerable-app.com/api/fetch?url=https://trusted.com%23@127.0.0.1:8080/admin"

# Application validates: host == "trusted.com" ✓ PASSES
# Application fetches: https://127.0.0.1:8080/admin
# Internal admin panel content returned to attacker
```

**Stage 3: Full Compromise**

Attacker can now:
- Fetch internal metadata services: `http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/`
- Access internal APIs without authentication
- Read database credentials from internal config services
- Enumerate and attack internal microservices
- Perform privilege escalation attacks

## Common Mistakes

1. **No rate limiting on sensitive operations** — Allowing unlimited password reset attempts, login tries, or API calls. Rate limiting is a design-level control that cannot be retrofitted easily.

2. **Trust boundaries not defined** — Not distinguishing between trusted internal services and untrusted user input. Every system boundary should validate and sanitize data.

3. **Business logic flaws** — Focusing on technical security (XSS, SQLi) while ignoring business logic issues like price manipulation, coupon stacking, or privilege escalation through workflow abuse.

4. **Missing threat modeling** — Shipping features without considering "what could go wrong?" Threat modeling catches design flaws before they become vulnerabilities.

## Secure Implementation

### Tier 1: Critical Emergency Fix (5 minutes)
**IMMEDIATE: Upgrade Spring Framework to patched version**

The vulnerability is in Spring Framework's URL parsing. Upgrade immediately:

```xml
<!-- pom.xml -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.framework</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-web</artifactId>
    <!-- VULNERABLE versions -->
    <!-- 5.3.0-5.3.31, 6.0.0-6.0.16, 6.1.0-6.1.3 -->

    <!-- PATCHED versions (use MINIMUM) -->
    <version>6.1.4</version>  <!-- Or 6.0.17, 5.3.32 -->
</dependency>

<!-- If using Spring Boot -->
<parent>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-parent</artifactId>
    <version>3.2.3</version>  <!-- Includes patched Spring 6.1.4+ -->
</parent>
```

Verify patch:

```bash
mvn dependency:tree | grep spring-web
# Must show 5.3.32+, 6.0.17+, or 6.1.4+
```

**Status: Tier 1 complete in <5 minutes. UriComponentsBuilder parsing is patched.**

